# 7. Load Balancing

## Definition

A load balancer distributes incoming traffic across multiple backend servers.

It provides clients with one stable endpoint while hiding backend instances.

```text
                     +--> Server A
Client --> LB -------+--> Server B
                     +--> Server C
```

## Responsibilities

A load balancer can:

- Receive incoming traffic
- Select a backend server
- Forward requests
- Return responses
- Perform health checks
- Remove unhealthy servers
- Restore recovered servers
- Support automatic failover
- Support blue-green deployments
- Support canary deployments
- Perform SSL termination
- Perform compression
- Apply rate limiting
- Provide WAF protection
- Protect backend servers from overload

## Why It Is Needed

Without a load balancer:

- Clients may need individual server addresses
- Traffic distribution becomes difficult
- Failed servers may continue receiving traffic
- Deployments become risky
- Horizontal scaling becomes difficult to manage

---

# 8. Health Checks

Health checks determine whether a backend server should receive traffic.

```text
Load Balancer
    |
    +--> Server A: Healthy   --> Receives traffic
    +--> Server B: Unhealthy --> Removed from rotation
    +--> Server C: Healthy   --> Receives traffic
```

## Liveness

Checks whether the application process is running.

## Readiness

Checks whether the application is ready to serve real requests.

A readiness check may verify:

- Application initialization
- Required internal resources
- Database connectivity
- Connection-pool health
- Required configuration
- Ability to process requests

## Common Mistake

A health check verifies only that the process is alive.

The process may be running while the application is unable to serve users correctly.

## Load Balancer Availability

A single load balancer can become a single point of failure.

Production systems should consider:

- Redundant load balancers
- Health monitoring
- Automatic failover
- Capacity planning
- Regional traffic routing

---

# 9. Layer 4 Load Balancing

Layer 4 operates at the transport layer.

It uses:

- IP address
- Port
- TCP information
- UDP information

## Advantages

- Fast
- Low processing overhead
- Suitable for high-throughput traffic
- Useful for connection-level routing

## Limitations

It cannot normally route using:

- URL paths
- HTTP methods
- HTTP headers
- Cookies
- Request bodies

---

# 10. Layer 7 Load Balancing

Layer 7 operates at the application layer.

It understands HTTP information such as:

- HTTP method
- URL path
- Headers
- Cookies
- Request content

## Example

```text
/api/orders/* --> Order Service
/api/users/*  --> User Service
/images/*     --> Media Service
/beta/*       --> Beta Servers
```

## Advantages

- Intelligent routing
- Path-based routing
- Header-based routing
- Cookie-based routing
- Routing beta users to beta servers
- Advanced deployment strategies

## Limitations

- More processing overhead
- More complex configuration
- Requires application-protocol awareness

## Layer 4 vs Layer 7

| Factor | Layer 4 | Layer 7 |
|---|---|---|
| Layer | Transport | Application |
| Uses | IP, port, TCP, and UDP | URL, method, headers, and cookies |
| Speed | Generally faster | More processing required |
| Routing intelligence | Limited | Advanced |
| HTTP awareness | No | Yes |
| Configuration | Simpler | More complex |
| Best for | Connection routing | Intelligent application routing |

---

# 11. Load-Balancing Algorithms

The algorithm determines how the load balancer chooses a backend server.

Main algorithms:

1. Round robin
2. Weighted round robin
3. Least connections
4. IP hash
5. Consistent hashing

---

# 12. Round Robin

Round robin sends requests to servers one by one in a repeating cycle.

```text
Request 1 --> Server A
Request 2 --> Server B
Request 3 --> Server C
Request 4 --> Server A
Request 5 --> Server B
```

## Use When

- Servers have similar capacity
- Requests have similar processing costs
- Predictable distribution is sufficient
- Simplicity is important

## Advantages

- Simple
- Predictable
- Easy to configure
- Easy to understand
- Easy to debug

## Limitation

Round robin distributes equal request counts, not equal amounts of work.

```text
Request 1 --> Fetch cached product
Request 2 --> Generate large PDF
Request 3 --> Upload large file
```

These requests require different amounts of CPU, memory, disk, and time.

> Equal request count does not mean equal server load.

---

# 13. Weighted Round Robin

Weighted round robin assigns different traffic shares based on server capacity or deployment strategy.

```text
Server A: Weight 4
Server B: Weight 2
Server C: Weight 1
```

Server A receives more traffic than Server B and Server C.

## Use When

- Servers have different capacities
- Instance types are different
- Regional capacity differs
- Traffic must shift gradually
- Canary deployments are required

## Canary Example

```text
Old Version: 95%
New Version: 5%
```

Traffic can be increased gradually:

```text
5% --> 25% --> 50% --> 95% --> 100%
```

Monitor:

- Error rates
- Latency
- Resource usage
- Business failures
- Application health

## Limitation

Weights are usually predefined.

Weighted round robin does not automatically understand live CPU, memory, latency, or server stress.

---

# 14. Least Connections

Least connections sends a new request to the server with the fewest active connections.

```text
Server A: 100 active connections
Server B: 40 active connections
Server C: 70 active connections

New request --> Server B
```

## Use When

- Requests have different durations
- WebSocket connections are used
- Streaming is involved
- Uploads take different amounts of time
- Connections are long-lived
- Workload duration is uneven

## Advantage

It considers active connections rather than only request count.

## Limitation

Connection count may not represent actual resource usage.

```text
Server A: 10 CPU-heavy connections
Server B: 20 lightweight connections
```

Server A may be more overloaded even though it has fewer connections.

---

# 15. IP Hash

IP hash uses the client's IP address to select a backend server.

```text
hash(clientIP) --> Backend Server
```

The same client IP generally returns to the same server.

## Purpose

It provides:

- Session affinity
- Sticky sessions

## Use When

- Session affinity is required
- A legacy application stores sessions locally
- The same client must return to the same server

## Problems

- Corporate NAT can place many users behind one IP
- One server may receive too much traffic
- Mobile users may change IP addresses
- Moving from Wi-Fi to mobile data may break affinity
- Adding or removing servers may remap many users
- Failover becomes harder

> IP hash can support legacy stateful behavior, but it should not replace proper stateless design.

---

# 16. Consistent Hashing

Consistent hashing maps servers and keys onto a logical hash ring.

```text
                Server A
                   |
        Key 1 -----|------ Key 2
       /                       \
Server D                         Server B
       \                       /
        Key 4 -----|------ Key 3
                   |
                Server C
```

When a server is added or removed, only part of the key space moves.

## Use When

- Stable routing is required
- Cache ownership matters
- Distributed storage is used
- Membership changes should cause minimal remapping
- Controlled redistribution is important

## Advantages

- Reduces remapping when servers change
- Useful for distributed caches
- Useful for distributed storage
- Supports stable key ownership

## Limitations

- More complex than simple hashing
- Uneven distribution can occur
- Virtual nodes may be required
- Requires careful balancing

---

# 17. Algorithm Comparison

| Algorithm | Best For | Main Risk |
|---|---|---|
| Round robin | Similar servers and requests | Ignores actual request cost |
| Weighted round robin | Different capacities and gradual rollouts | Uses static weights |
| Least connections | Long-lived or uneven requests | Connections may not equal load |
| IP hash | Session affinity | Imbalance and remapping |
| Consistent hashing | Stable routing and cache ownership | Additional complexity |

## Selection Guide

```text
Similar servers + similar requests
    --> Round robin

Different server capacities
    --> Weighted round robin

Long-lived or uneven connections
    --> Least connections

Simple client affinity
    --> IP hash

Stable key ownership during scaling
    --> Consistent hashing
```

There is no universally best algorithm. Choose based on workload characteristics.

