# 18. Stateless Service Design

## Definition

A stateless service does not keep required user-specific state inside one server's local memory or local disk between requests.

Each request either:

- Contains the required information
- Contains a token identifying the user
- Retrieves state from shared storage

## Stateful Problem

```text
User logs in
    |
    v
Session stored in Server A
    |
    v
Next request reaches Server B
    |
    v
Server B does not know the user
```

This may force the system to use sticky sessions.

## Stateless Design

```text
                 +--> Server A
User --> LB -----+--> Server B
                 +--> Server C
                         |
                         v
                 Shared State Store
                 Redis / Database
```

Any server can retrieve the required state.

## Moving State Outside Servers

| State | Better Location |
|---|---|
| Authentication identity | Token |
| Session data | Redis or shared session store |
| Shopping cart | Database or distributed cache |
| Uploaded files | Object storage |
| Background tasks | Queue |
| Business data | Database |
| Logs | Centralized logging system |
| Temporary work | Queue or shared storage |
| Configuration | External configuration system |

## Key Principle

```text
Stateful design creates server dependency.

Stateless design creates server replaceability.
```

## Important Clarification

Stateless does not mean the system has no state.

It means:

> The individual application server does not own required user-specific state locally between requests.

---

# 19. Stateful vs Stateless Services

| Factor | Stateful Service | Stateless Service |
|---|---|---|
| User state | Stored locally | Stored externally or passed with request |
| Request routing | May require the same server | Any server can handle the request |
| Sticky sessions | Often required | Usually unnecessary |
| Server failure | Can lose user state | State remains in shared systems |
| Scaling | Harder | Easier |
| Failover | Difficult | Cleaner |
| Deployment | Riskier | Easier |
| Replaceability | Low | High |

## Stateful Examples

- Session object in server memory
- Cart stored in local RAM
- Uploaded file stored on local disk
- Local cache required for correctness

## Stateless Examples

- Identity represented using a token
- Session stored in Redis
- Cart stored in a database
- File stored in object storage
- Work placed on a shared queue

---

# 20. Statelessness Checklist

Before horizontally scaling a service, verify:

- [ ] Sessions are stored outside application servers
- [ ] Uploaded files use shared or object storage
- [ ] User identity uses tokens or shared sessions
- [ ] Any server can process any request
- [ ] Local cache is optional rather than required for correctness
- [ ] Background tasks use shared queues
- [ ] Scheduled jobs are coordinated
- [ ] Logs are centralized
- [ ] Configuration is externalized
- [ ] A server can fail without losing critical user state
- [ ] New instances can join without manual state migration
- [ ] Traffic can move away from a failed server safely

If several answers are `No`, the service is not ready for clean horizontal scaling.

