# 21. Service Discovery

## Definition

Service discovery allows services to find the current healthy instances of another service.

It solves the problem of dynamic service locations.

## Why It Is Needed

Instances can:

- Start
- Stop
- Crash
- Restart
- Move
- Scale up
- Scale down
- Receive new IP addresses

Hard-coded IP addresses cannot manage these changes reliably.

## Basic Flow

```text
1. A service instance starts
2. The instance registers itself
3. The registry stores its address and health
4. A caller requests the target service location
5. The registry returns healthy instances
6. The caller or proxy selects one instance
```

## Visual Flow

```text
Service A Instance 1 ----+
Service A Instance 2 ----+--> Service Registry
Service A Instance 3 ----+

Service B --> Registry --> Healthy Service A Instances
```

## Service Registry Information

A service registry may contain:

- Service name
- Instance address
- Port
- Health status
- Availability
- Metadata

## When It Is Important

- Multiple services exist
- Services have multiple instances
- Autoscaling is enabled
- Containers are used
- Instances change frequently
- IP addresses are dynamic
- Services communicate with one another

---

# 22. Client-Side Discovery

The calling service queries the registry and selects an instance itself.

```text
Client Service
      |
      v
Service Registry
      |
      v
Healthy Instances
      |
      v
Client selects one instance
```

## Advantages

- Fewer network hops
- Can use local caching
- Client can make routing decisions
- Direct communication with the selected instance

## Disadvantages

- Every client needs discovery logic
- Language-specific libraries may be required
- Routing logic becomes distributed
- Client maintenance becomes harder

---

# 23. Server-Side Discovery

The client calls a load balancer, proxy, or gateway.

That component discovers and selects the backend instance.

```text
Client
   |
   v
Load Balancer / Proxy
   |
   +--> Service Registry
   |
   v
Healthy Service Instance
```

## Advantages

- Simpler clients
- Discovery logic is centralized
- Language-agnostic
- Easier operational control
- Routing policies are managed centrally

## Disadvantages

- Adds another network hop
- Proxy becomes critical infrastructure
- Can add latency
- Can become a bottleneck if poorly designed

## Comparison

| Factor | Client-Side | Server-Side |
|---|---|---|
| Instance selection | Client | Proxy or load balancer |
| Client complexity | Higher | Lower |
| Network hops | Fewer | Additional proxy hop |
| Language dependency | Possible | Lower |
| Routing control | Distributed | Centralized |
| Operational management | Harder | Easier |

---

# 24. DNS Discovery vs Service Registry

## DNS Discovery

A service name resolves to one or more IP addresses.

```text
payment-service.internal
        |
        v
10.0.1.10
10.0.1.11
10.0.1.12
```

### Good For

- Simple environments
- Kubernetes-style service names
- Stable internal routing
- Basic service lookup

### Limitations

- DNS caching can delay updates
- Stale entries may remain temporarily
- Health awareness may be limited
- Advanced routing is harder

## Dedicated Service Registry

A dedicated registry tracks service instances and health.

### Good For

- Dynamic microservices
- Autoscaling environments
- Frequent instance changes
- Client-side discovery
- Rich health and metadata requirements

### Limitations

- Another system must be operated
- Registry availability becomes important
- Clients or proxies must integrate with it
- Stale registration data must be handled

